{"id": "450", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression caused by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava gets emptied. This cavity creates pressure and the overground rocks collapse to create a large depression. On the other hand, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for the eruption of magma and lava. This is a depression that is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure and it typically has the opening through which lava and ash flows upwards. Craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression formed when a magma chamber collapses, creating a large cavity underground that causes overlying rocks to collapse. It is at least one kilometer across and is associated with volcanic activity. In contrast, a crater is a bowl-shaped depression at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. Craters can also be caused by meteorite impacts and vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression formed when a magma chamber collapses, creating a large cavity underground that causes overlying rocks to collapse. It is at least one kilometer across and is associated with volcanic activity. In contrast, a crater is a bowl-shaped depression at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. Craters can also be caused by meteorite impacts and vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "time_sec": 17.859, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 266