{"id": "508", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Porterhouse steaks are cut from further back on the sirloin and contain more of the tenderloin or fillet than sirloin steaks. They are essentially a larger version of the T-Bone steak, carved from a larger portion of the tenderloin. On the other hand, the sirloin steak is a steak cut from the back of the animal, continuing off the short loin from which T-bone, porterhouse, and club steaks are cut. The sirloin is divided into several different types of steak.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is a general term for a steak cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin that includes a large portion of the tenderloin and a T-shaped bone, making it a larger version of the T-bone steak.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is a general term for a steak cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin that includes a large portion of the tenderloin and a T-shaped bone, making it a larger version of the T-bone steak.", "time_sec": 15.65, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 323, 87186, 7675, 48959, 374, 429, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 374, 264, 4586, 4647, 369, 264, 48959, 3931, 504, 279, 1182, 315, 279, 9864, 11, 11689, 279, 14639, 1182, 13348, 14354, 1007, 279, 2805, 76960, 11, 1393, 87186, 7675, 48959, 374, 264, 3151, 3931, 504, 279, 2805, 76960, 429, 5646, 264, 3460, 13348, 315, 279, 27582, 385, 258, 323, 264, 350, 34731, 17240, 11, 3259, 432, 264, 8131, 2319, 315, 279, 350, 1455, 603, 48959, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0181, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.6523, -0.5001, -0.1154, -0.0132, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0044, -0.0, -0.0385, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1603, -0.007, -0.2644, 0.0, 0.0, -0.051, -0.0026, -0.6937, -1.0967, -0.2309, -0.0057, -0.3649, -0.1621, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4759, -0.1688, -0.0, -0.0033, -0.0504, -0.1394, -0.4305, -0.0012, -0.0388, -0.0, -0.1009, -0.1602, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2669, -0.3755, -0.1162, -0.0, -0.0031, -0.0008, -0.0021, -0.015, -0.3014, -0.2228, -0.0067, -0.021, -0.0, -0.6445, -0.066, -0.255, -0.7047, -0.0386, -0.0, -0.0662, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4464, -0.6452, -0.4733, -0.0021, -0.0011, -0.7061, -0.2266, -0.0002, -0.764, -0.3453, -0.1426, -0.0318, -0.0124, -0.0044, -0.0125, -0.0, -0.0265, -0.3935, -1.0337], "cumulative_logprob": -14.3261, "norm_logp": -0.1592, "input_len": 657, "output_tokens": 90, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 51.06, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "519", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket having a unique number. In a reverse raffle, tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. Tickets are sold at $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals collectively buys one $1000 ticket. There are also additional prizes like $500 early bird and first ticket pulled prizes. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% supports a charity. Some raffles, like cash tip board raffles, offer additional chances to win, such as $10,000, and are not included in the ticket price.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket having a unique number. In a reverse raffle, tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. Tickets are sold at $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals collectively buys one $1000 ticket. There are also additional prizes like $500 early bird and first ticket pulled prizes. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% supports a charity. Some raffles, like cash tip board raffles, offer additional chances to win, such as $10,000, and are not included in the ticket price.", "time_sec": 28.489, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 435, 80535, 975, 553, 11236, 14403, 311, 13026, 11, 448, 1817, 11727, 3432, 264, 4911, 1372, 13, 758, 264, 9931, 435, 44090, 11, 14403, 525, 14764, 825, 553, 825, 11, 323, 279, 1537, 9664, 11727, 14816, 13, 35615, 525, 6088, 518, 400, 20, 15, 1817, 11, 323, 264, 1965, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 44825, 49531, 825, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 11727, 13, 2619, 525, 1083, 5107, 37408, 1075, 400, 20, 15, 15, 4124, 11958, 323, 1156, 11727, 13238, 37408, 13, 758, 264, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 4038, 11, 14403, 525, 6088, 2337, 458, 1538, 11, 323, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 2790, 6625, 728, 311, 279, 13632, 315, 264, 4194, 4038, 11, 1393, 279, 1008, 220, 20, 15, 4, 11554, 264, 22846, 13, 4329, 435, 80535, 11, 1075, 8350, 11552, 4479, 435, 80535, 11, 3010, 5107, 16963, 311, 3164, 11, 1741, 438, 400, 16, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 11, 323, 525, 537, 5230, 304, 279, 11727, 3349, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0048, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0148, -0.0, -0.1198, -0.1566, -0.0119, -0.4896, -0.2062, -0.0176, -0.9262, -0.5563, -0.5243, -0.0013, -0.8861, -0.