{"id": "548", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. As an amplifier, it takes a small electric current at one end (input current) and produces a larger electric current (output current) at the other end. As a switch, it uses a small amount of electricity to control a gate on a larger supply of electricity. The transistor works when the electrons and the holes start moving across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon. The current passes from the emitter to the collector through the base. The amount of collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small input current and produces a much larger output current. When used as a switch, it controls the flow of current between the emitter and collector. In an NPN transistor, the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to its more positive voltage, allowing current to flow from emitter to collector. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, with β typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors. Transistors work when electrons and holes move across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small input current and produces a much larger output current. When used as a switch, it controls the flow of current between the emitter and collector. In an NPN transistor, the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to its more positive voltage, allowing current to flow from emitter to collector. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, with β typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors. Transistors work when electrons and holes move across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "time_sec": 32.447, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 11, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 1084, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 1946, 1482, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 8131, 2550, 1482, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 432, 11574, 279, 6396, 315, 1482, 1948, 279, 47263, 323, 31953, 13, 758, 458, 451, 17896, 97941, 11, 279, 2331, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 4152, 311, 1181, 803, 6785, 21720, 11, 10693, 1482, 311, 6396, 504, 47263, 311, 31953, 13, 576, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 11, 448, 33218, 11136, 7046, 1091, 220, 16, 15, 15, 369, 2613, 1331, 25719, 1356, 380, 1087, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 975, 979, 56578, 323, 19766, 3271, 3941, 279, 48241, 82, 1948, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 26048, 2070, 23067, 1356, 380, 1087, 11, 279, 425, 81181, 374,