{"id": "644", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, first cut large potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, being careful not to break the long sprouts. Place these chunks on a tray and let them dry out in a cool, dark place overnight. Small, sprouted potatoes can be planted whole. Next, prepare your planting area by turning the soil over with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches, removing any large rocks or debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut your potatoes into cubes of about 1 to 2 inches, making sure to leave a sprout on each cube. Place these cubes in the trench, sprouted side facing up, spaced about 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover the potatoes with compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the potatoes thoroughly and regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Finally, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, keeping it at least 4 inches away from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch will help keep the soil cool and maintain a consistent moisture level.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, first cut large sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts are not broken. Place the chunks on a tray and let them dry in a cool, dark place overnight. For planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches and break it finely, removing rocks and debris. Dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing multiple rows 24 to 36 inches apart. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted side facing up, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with compost and then soil. Water thoroughly and maintain moisture with regular watering and a 4-inch mulch layer, keeping mulch 4 inches away from the vines.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, to plant a sprouted potato, first cut large sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, ensuring the long sprouts are not broken. Place the chunks on a tray and let them dry in a cool, dark place overnight. For planting, turn the soil to a depth of 12 inches and break it finely, removing rocks and debris. Dig a trench 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, spacing multiple rows 24 to 36 inches apart. Place the potato chunks in the trench with the sprouted side facing up, spacing them 6 to 8 inches apart. Cover with compost and then soil. Water thoroughly and maintain moisture with regular watering and a 4-inch mulch layer, keeping mulch 4 inches away from the vines.", "time_sec": 28.234, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1156, 3931, 3460, 8151, 18533, 34167, 1119, 26757, 448, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 1817, 11, 22573, 279, 1293, 8151, 11672, 525, 537, 10865, 13, 10771, 279, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 323, 1077, 1105, 9058, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 1992, 24398, 13, 1752, 47014, 11, 2484, 279, 17171, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 1438, 432, 60702, 11, 17592, 23035, 323, 33998, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 11, 25943, 5248, 6978, 220, 17, 19, 311, 220, 18, 21, 14924, 10747, 13, 10771, 279, 38734, 26757, 304, 279, 53252, 448, 279, 8151, 18533, 3108, 12880, 705, 11, 25943, 1105, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 10747, 13, 17757, 448, 56723, 323, 1221, 17171, 13, 9959, 26364, 323, 10306, 31157, 448, 5792, 72741, 323, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 15602, 331, 6193, 11, 10282, 15602, 331, 220, 19, 14924, 3123, 504, 279, 85429, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0014, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0993, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.2813, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0568, -0.26, -0.0329, -0.141, -0.2456, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.1138, -0.0057, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.007, -0.1228, -0.5366, -0.5139, -0.4941, -0.0, -0.0, -0.7043, -0.0091, -0.0004, -0.1