{"id": "211", "question": "differences between elements compounds and mixtures", "gt": "Elements are the purest form of a chemical substance and are composed of only one type of atom, with all atoms having the same number of protons. Compounds, on the other hand, are substances composed of two or more different elements and are also pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded, allowing for easy separation of the components using physical methods.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, elements are the purest form of a chemical substance composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together and are pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the components are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures have variable composition, show the properties of their constituents, and can be separated easily by physical methods. In contrast, compounds have fixed composition and require chemical methods to separate their elements.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, elements are the purest form of a chemical substance composed of only one type of atom. Compounds are substances made of two or more different elements chemically bonded together and are pure substances. Mixtures are physical combinations of elements or compounds where the components are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity. Mixtures have variable composition, show the properties of their constituents, and can be separated easily by physical methods. In contrast, compounds have fixed composition and require chemical methods to separate their elements.", "time_sec": 18.121, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 5424, 525, 279, 10526,