{"id": "550", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is an electronic component made of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It functions as an amplifier or a switch depending on the flow of electrons through its base. When working as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current at one end and produces a larger electric current at the other. The flow of electrons is controlled by using a small amount of electricity to turn a valve-like structure in the transistor. In its off state, the holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector. Transistors can have common-emitter configuration where collector current is approximately β times the base current.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small input current and produces a much larger output current. When used as a switch, it controls the flow of current between the emitter and collector. In an NPN transistor, the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to its more positive voltage, allowing current to flow from emitter to collector. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, with β typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors. Transistors work when electrons and holes move across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a transistor is a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power, composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit. It can function as an amplifier or a switch. When used as an amplifier, it takes in a small input current and produces a much larger output current. When used as a switch, it controls the flow of current between the emitter and collector. In an NPN transistor, the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to its more positive voltage, allowing current to flow from emitter to collector. The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, with β typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors. Transistors work when electrons and holes move across the junctions between n-type and p-type silicon. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT is a low-input-impedance device.", "time_sec": 32.818, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 97941, 374, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 11, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 13, 1084, 646, 729, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 1946, 1482, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 8131, 2550, 1482, 13, 3197, 1483, 438, 264, 3398, 11, 432, 11574, 279, 6396, 315, 1482, 1948, 279, 47263, 323, 31953, 13, 758, 458, 451, 17896, 97941, 11, 279, 2331, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 4152, 311, 1181, 803, 6785, 21720, 11, 10693, 1482, 311, 6396, 504, 47263, 311, 31953, 13, 576, 31953, 1482, 374, 13187, 33218, 320, 5464, 36512, 3248, 1482, 8722, 8, 3039, 279, 2331, 1482, 11, 448, 33218, 11136, 7046, 1091, 220, 16, 15, 15, 369, 2613, 1331, 25719, 1356, 380, 1087, 13, 4058, 380, 1087, 975, 979, 56578, 323, 19766, 3271, 3941, 279, 48241, 82, 1948, 308, 10604, 323, 281, 10604, 50592, 13, 26048, 2070, 23067, 1356, 380, 1087, 11, 279, 425,