{"id": "351", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "gt": "Company goodwill is a business asset that is created when a business provides good service or product to their customers, leading to customer trust. This trust results in repeat customers and positive word-of-mouth, which can increase a company's customer base and retain old clients. Goodwill represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation of the business possesses. It is important because it can attract investors and future buyers, and it is an advantage of the reputation and connection with customers which tends to make that connection permanent.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, company goodwill is the value derived from a business's reputation and the positive relationships it has built with customers. It is created when customers trust the business due to good service or products, leading them to become repeat customers and recommend the business to others. Goodwill is important because it helps increase the customer base, retain existing clients, attract investors, and appeal to future buyers. It represents the lasting attraction customers feel toward a business because of its name and reputation, making it a valuable business asset.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, company goodwill is the value derived from a business's reputation and the positive relationships it has built with customers. It is created when customers trust the business due to good service or products, leading them to become repeat customers and recommend the business to others. Goodwill is important because it helps increase the customer base, retain existing clients, attract investors, and appeal to future buyers. It represents the lasting attraction customers feel toward a business because of its name and reputation, making it a valuable business asset.", "time_sec": 18.471, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 2813, 81646, 374, 279, 897, 14257, 504, 264, 2562, 594, 17011, 323, 279, 6785, 11871, 432, 702, 5798, 448, 6310, 13, 1084, 374, 3465, 979, 6310, 6950, 279, 2562, 4152, 311, 1661, 2473, 476, 3871, 11, 6388, 1105, 311, 3635, 13153, 6310, 323, 6934, 279, 2562, 311, 3800, 13, 7684, 14387, 374, 2989, 1576, 432, 8609, 5263, 279, 6002, 2331, 11, 14060, 6350, 8239, 11, 9320, 14823, 11, 323, 14303, 311, 3853, 22630, 13, 1084, 10868, 279, 28769, 32364, 6310, 2666, 8841, 264, 2562, 1576, 315, 1181, 829, 323, 17011, 11, 3259, 432, 264, 15172, 2562, 9329, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0197, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.005, -0.0, -0.5766, -0.0334, -0.6915, -0.042, -0.0, -0.0705, -0.0116, -0.0266, -0.2191, -0.0337, -0.6608, -0.2024, -0.8216, -0.0616, -0.8585, -0.2776, -0.0, -0.252, -0.665, -0.052, -0.9038, -0.1988, -0.0008, -0.2212, -0.2082, -0.1188, -0.1005, -0.0524, -0.0, -0.0507, -0.2752, -0.0004, -0.2358, -0.1282, -0.0677, -0.5759, -0.0, -0.0899, -0.0002, -0.5068, -0.0002, -0.0952, -0.0435, -0.0328, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.005, -0.1246, -0.0001, -0.1104, -0.0021, -0.0089, -0.0, -0.3209, -0.1032, -0.1081, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0383, -0.5902, -0.0432, -0.0298, -0.0, -0.0394, -0.0129, -0.0022, -0.0013, -0.3106, -0.0, -0.4743, -0.0008, -0.0739, -0.201, -0.4636, -0.0043, -0.7818, -0.3933, -0.3331, -0.3141, -0.1244, -0.5759, -0.1276, -1.2322, -0.0001, -0.0004, -0.149, -0.0206, -0.025, -0.54, -0.9479, -0.0486, -0.0706, -0.1561, -0.3461, -0.0, -0.1189, -0.0041], "cumulative_logprob": -18.8691, "norm_logp": -0.1797, "input_len": 509, "output_tokens": 105, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 61.95, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "358", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is that ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, specifically attacking a person's integrity or intelligence to redirect the discussion towards their faults.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument instead of addressing the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented, aiming to discredit the person's integrity or intelligence in advance. While both fallacies target the individual rather than the argument, poisoning the well is distinguished by its preemptive nature, attempting to undermine the person's credibility before any argument is made.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument instead of addressing the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented, aiming to discredit the person's integrity or intelligence in advance. While both fallacies target the individual rather than the argument, poisoning the well is distinguished by its preemptive nature, attempting to undermine the person's credibility before any argument is made.", "time_sec": 18.929, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 264, 1697, 3259, 458, 5693, 4518, 315, 27020, 279, 5693, 5086, 13, 576, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 264, 3151, 943, 315, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 429, 13666, 1573, 279, 5693, 374, 1496, 10449, 11, 37078, 311, 87019, 279, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 304, 11912, 13, 5976, 2176, 4399, 26030, 2169, 279, 3842, 4751, 1091, 279, 5693, 11, 51136, 279, 1632, 374, 38475, 553, 1181, 81747, 533, 6993, 11, 19405, 311, 41947, 279, 1697, 594, 37669, 1573, 894, 5693, 374, 1865, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0022, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.1994, -0.1519, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.6097, -0.0141, -0.1016, -0.0301, -0.0012, -0.0003, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.4739, -0.3974, -0.0, -0.2691, -0.0, -0.0933, -0.0032, -0.0, -0.4781, -0.0, -0.0154, -0.0076, -0.0001, -0.1335, -0.6384, -0.3465, -0.0182, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0043, -0.0084, -0.4977, -0.0659, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.068, -0.2204, -0.0537, -0.0218, -0.3407, -0.6298, -0.0277, -0.7804, -0.1522, -0.4719, -0.5245, -0.0001, -0.4086, -0.6296, -0.1061, -0.4919, -0.0359, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.4053, -0.0009, -0.3984, -0.357, -0.2219, -0.7656, -0.1055, -1.0947, -0.1373, -0.5791, -0.0163, -0.0, -0.0905, -0.011, -0.0089, -0.1553, -0.0, -0.0, -1.037, -0.5971, -0.0035, -0.2141, -0.239, -0.0, -0.007, -0.3527, -1.465, -0.0, -0.6302, -0.7167, -0.0827, -0.8943, -0.0034, -0.2173, -0.9527, -0.9236, -0.2627, -0.5609, -0.1339, -0.0274], "cumulative_logprob": -24.1951, "norm_logp": -0.224, "input_len": 459, "output_tokens": 108, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.91, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "367", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust that is normally exposed on the surface or underlying the soil. On the other hand, a stone is described as a smaller piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. The passages also mention that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, while rocks cannot. Therefore, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size, with rocks being larger and stones being smaller and easier to handle.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone lies in their size and usage. A rock is a large piece of stone that is difficult to carry in the hand, while a stone is a smaller piece or pebble that can be easily carried. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot. Additionally, the term \"rock\" is often used metaphorically to represent something solid and substantial, such as a foundation or a symbol of strength, whereas \"stone\" more frequently implies human use or manipulation.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between a rock and a stone lies in their size and usage. A rock is a large piece of stone that is difficult to carry in the hand, while a stone is a smaller piece or pebble that can be easily carried. Rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot. Additionally, the term \"rock\" is often used metaphorically to represent something solid and substantial, such as a foundation or a symbol of strength, whereas \"stone\" more frequently implies human use or manipulation.", "time_sec": 20.357, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 26