{"id": "443", "question": "how are forest fires beneficial to conifers like jack pines?", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines in several ways:\n\n1. Fire helps to clear the way for new growth by consuming dead, decaying vegetation on the forest floor, creating a clean slate for seed germination and seedling establishment (Passage 2).\n2. Jack pine seeds are adapted to fire, as they are stored in serotinous cones that only open after a fire has melted the resin holding them closed. This allows the seeds to disperse and establish new populations (Passage 2).\n3. Fire can create a favorable environment for jack pine and aspen regrowth by reducing competition from other tree species, such as spruce, which have better access to sunlight while shading the other trees (Passage 3).\n\nTherefore, forest fires can benefit jack pines by providing a means of seed dispersal and creating conditions that promote their growth and regeneration.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they trigger the release of seeds from serotinous cones that are filled with resin. These cones remain closed and dormant until a fire melts the resin, causing the cones to open and disperse the seeds. This fire-dependent mechanism allows jack pines to regenerate and proliferate after a fire, giving them a competitive advantage in disturbed areas.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, forest fires are beneficial to conifers like jack pines because they trigger the release of seeds from serotinous cones that are filled with resin. These cones remain closed and dormant until a fire melts the resin, causing the cones to open and disperse the seeds. This fire-dependent mechanism allows jack pines to regenerate and proliferate after a fire, giving them a competitive advantage in disturbed areas.", "time_sec": 15.414, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 13638, 26084, 525, 23699, 311, 390, 98813, 1075, 25072, 281, 1543, 1576, 807, 8183, 279, 4879, 315, 19056, 504, 1420, 354, 60698, 86892, 429, 525, 10199, 448, 53514, 13, 4220, 86892, 7146, 7877, 323, 88360, 3080, 264, 3940, 98091, 279, 53514, 11, 14381, 279, 86892, 311, 1787, 323, 827, 81244, 279, 19056, 13, 1096, 3940, 42818, 16953, 6147, 25072, 281, 1543, 311, 79451, 323, 41936, 349, 1283, 264, 3940, 11, 7086, 1105, 264, 14680, 9423, 304, 55407, 5671, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0069, -0.0, -0.0625, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0022, -0.0003, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.011, -0.0, -0.0804, -0.4659, -1.1737, -0.2843, -0.1287, -0.0007, -0.0059, -0.3473, -0.0784, -0.0006, -0.0001, -0.0047, -0.7158, -0.7068, -0.1654, -0.0, -0.0055, -0.5435, -0.2175, -0.0432, -0.0426, -0.0281, -0.43, -0.0018, -0.006, -0.1451, -0.0001, -0.1881, -0.0, -0.0, -0.005, -0.1608, -0.1602, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0182, -0.003, -0.068, -0.0036, -0.7865, -0.0, -0.0522, -0.686, -1.2154, -0.109, -0.6484, -0.2374, -0.0027, -0.3869, -0.0, -0.0098, -0.0778, -0.5213, -0.5296, -0.0, -0.2778, -0.5957, -0.0304, -0.8478, -0.3183, -0.0001, -0.2824, -0.0644, -0.0022, -0.194, -1.1148, -0.901, -0.2381, -1.0636], "cumulative_logprob": -17.5075, "norm_logp": -0.2036, "input_len": 549, "output_tokens": 86, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.21, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "452", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression made due to volcanic activity, while a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano that allows for the eruption of magma and lava. The primary difference between the two is the method of formation. Calderas are formed when a magma chamber collapses, resulting in a large depression, while craters are formed by impacts from meteorites.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression formed when a magma chamber collapses, creating a large cavity underground that causes overlying rocks to collapse. It is at least one kilometer across and is associated with volcanic activity. In contrast, a crater is a bowl-shaped depression at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. Craters can also be caused by meteorite impacts and vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, a caldera is a large depression formed when a magma chamber collapses, creating a large cavity underground that causes overlying rocks to collapse. It is at least one kilometer across and is associated with volcanic activity. In contrast, a crater is a bowl-shaped depression at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed by the sinking of rocks due to high pressure. Craters can also be caused by meteorite impacts and vary in size from very small to several hundred kilometers across.", "time_sec": 18.301, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 264, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 18210, 14122, 979, 264, 63505, 24220, 86358, 11, 6825, 264, 3460, 55329, 25260, 429, 11137, 916, 6711, 23035, 311, 18179, 13, 1084, 374, 518, 3245, 825, 15045, 20408, 3941, 323, 