{"id": "503", "question": "factors that cause oily skin", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main factors that contribute to oily skin are:\n\n1. Genetics: According to passage 1, oily skin is hereditary, meaning that if one's parents have oily skin, they are more likely to experience it themselves.\n2. Hormonal shifts: Passage 1 suggests that hormonal changes in the body can cause oily skin to come and go.\n3. Diet: While diet can aggravate acne, it does not directly cause oily skin, according to passage 1.\n4. Poor hygiene: Passage 2 mentions that poor hygiene and topical applications can contribute to oily skin.\n5. Combination skin: Passage 3 defines different skin types, including combination skin, which can be prone to oily skin in certain areas, such as the T-zone.\n\nThere is no additional information provided in the passages to suggest other factors that might contribute to oily skin. Therefore, the answer to the question is based on the information provided in the passages.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. Oily skin is hereditary, meaning if a parent has oily skin, their child is likely to have it as well. While diet and lifestyle changes may not prevent oily skin, they can aggravate acne. Contrary to popular belief, oily foods and alcohol have limited effects on skin oiliness. Hormonal changes may also influence oily skin, especially if the condition comes and goes.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. Oily skin is hereditary, meaning if a parent has oily skin, their child is likely to have it as well. While diet and lifestyle changes may not prevent oily skin, they can aggravate acne. Contrary to popular belief, oily foods and alcohol have limited effects on skin oiliness. Hormonal changes may also influence oily skin, especially if the condition comes and goes.", "time_sec": 17.007, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 9363, 429, 5240, 77471, 6787, 2924, 55004, 323, 73118, 28635, 13, 506, 1541, 6787, 374, 1059, 94354, 11, 7290, 421, 264, 2681, 702, 77471, 6787, 11, 862, 1682, 374, 4363, 311, 614, 432, 438, 1632, 13, 5976, 9968, 323, 18899, 4344, 1231, 537, 5358, 77471, 6787, 11, 807, 646, 44941, 349, 45805, 13, 34241, 658, 311, 5411, 16396, 11, 77471, 15298, 323, 12904, 614, 7199, 6239, 389, 6787, 5590, 1880, 13, 91108, 24202, 4344, 1231, 1083, 10173, 77471, 6787, 11, 5310, 421, 279, 2971, 4041, 323, 5780, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.4334, -0.001, -0.0013, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0869, -0.1019, -0.4783, -0.0053, -0.0018, -0.0127, -0.0372, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.1568, -0.0, -0.0049, -0.0245, -0.091, -0.7564, -0.3138, -0.0007, -0.2813, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1346, -0.5891, -0.0631, -0.1604, -0.0, -0.0367, -0.0005, -0.3871, -0.0, -0.0516, -0.74, -0.0455, -0.7979, -0.7154, -1.3802, -0.7758, -0.57, -0.966, -0.1194, -0.0, -0.2693, -0.8258, -0.0446, -0.7396, -0.0, -0.4693, -0.6658, -1.6927, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.5346, -0.0359, -0.0004, -1.1847, -0.0739, -1.1566, -0.024, -0.4394, -0.644, -0.2129, -0.0099, -0.1749, -0.0018, -0.0299, -0.0538, -0.3164, -0.0001, -0.5592, -1.0933, -0.3798, -0.7846, -0.2613, -0.0, -0.0948, -0.5995, -0.2332, -0.6552, -0.609, -0.6389, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2688, -1.1268], "cumulative_logprob": -27.2286, "norm_logp": -0.2866, "input_len": 712, "output_tokens": 95, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.9, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.0, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "510", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that the main difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is their location within the sirloin muscle. Sirloin steaks are cut from the back of the animal and continue off the short loin from which other cuts such as T-bone and porterhouse steaks are made. On the other hand, porterhouse steaks are specifically carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin and therefore have more of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-bone steaks. However, the passages suggest that both sirloin and porterhouse steaks may be referred to as the same thing or as a large version of the T-bone due to their similarity in appearance and taste.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is a general term for a steak cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin that includes a large portion of the tenderloin and a T-shaped bone, making it a larger version of the T-bone steak.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that sirloin steak is a general term for a steak cut from the back of the animal, specifically the rear back portion continuing off the short loin, while porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin that includes a large portion of the tenderloin and a T-shaped bone, making it a larger version of the T-bone steak.", "time_sec": 16.243, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 279, 6672, 1948, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 323, 87186, 7675, 48959, 374, 429, 27048, 385, 258, 48959, 374, 264, 4586, 4647, 369, 264, 48959, 3931, 