{"id": "521", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "pred": "Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket having a unique number. In a reverse raffle, tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. Tickets are sold at $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals collectively buys one $1000 ticket. There are also additional prizes like $500 early bird and first ticket pulled prizes. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% supports a charity. Some raffles, like cash tip board raffles, offer additional chances to win, such as $10,000, and are not included in the ticket price.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_model_qwen3_omni", "raw_output": "Based on the given passages, raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket having a unique number. In a reverse raffle, tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. Tickets are sold at $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals collectively buys one $1000 ticket. There are also additional prizes like $500 early bird and first ticket pulled prizes. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% supports a charity. Some raffles, like cash tip board raffles, offer additional chances to win, such as $10,000, and are not included in the ticket price.", "time_sec": 29.667, "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 435, 80535, 975, 553, 11236, 14403, 311, 13026, 11, 448, 1817, 11727, 3432, 264, 4911, 1372, 13, 758, 264, 9931, 435, 44090, 11, 14403, 525, 14764, 825, 553, 825, 11, 323, 279, 1537, 9664, 11727, 14816, 13, 35615, 525, 6088, 518, 400, 20, 15, 1817, 11, 323, 264, 1965, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 44825, 49531, 825, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 11727, 13, 2619, 525, 1083, 5107, 37408, 1075, 400, 20, 15, 15, 4124, 11958, 323, 1156, 11727, 13238, 37408, 13, 758, 264, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 4038, 11, 14403, 525, 6088, 2337, 458, 1538, 11, 323, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 2790, 6625, 728, 311, 279, 13632, 315, 264, 4194, 4038, 11, 1393, 279, 1008, 220, 20, 15, 4, 11554, 264, 22846, 13, 4329, 435, 80535, 11, 1075, 8350, 11552, 4479, 435, 80535, 11, 3010, 5107, 16963, 311, 3164, 11, 1741, 438, 400, 16, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 11, 323, 525, 537, 5230, 304, 279, 11727, 3349, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0048, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0148, -0.0, -0.1198, -0.1566, -0.0119, -0.4896, -0.2062, -0.0176, -0.9262, -0.5563, -0.5243, -0.0013, -0.8861, -0.0106, -0.3004, -0.0424, -0.5934, -0.0615, -0.021, -0.0133, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0023, -0.4769, -0.2744, -0.8545, -0.1834, -0.2541, -0.0, -0.2454, -0.0242, -0.1815, -0.6347, -0.4016, -0.0153, -0.0844, -0.5185, -1.1368, -1.0071, -1.1147, -0.5504, -0.2691, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0028, -0.2241, -0.2571, -0.2613, -0.2707, -0.0638, -0.127, -0.0, 0.0, -0.2572, -1.1319, -1.2171, -0.1004, -0.5571, -0.0, -0.062, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0409, -0.7969, -1.1545, -0.1674, -0.2105, -1.2624, -0.6485, -0.8011, -1.1282, -0.0036, -